WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:17.742
Imagine you get twenty pounds pocket money a month, but you spend twenty-five. You are five pounds short, so you must borrow. These are example figures. A government faces the same sum, only much bigger.

00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:28.608
Government revenue is the money the government receives, mostly from taxes. Government expenditure is what it spends, for example on health, schools and benefits.

00:00:29.160 --> 00:00:41.925
The fiscal balance is revenue minus expenditure. If revenue is greater than spending, the answer is positive: a fiscal surplus. If spending is greater than revenue, the answer is negative: a fiscal deficit.

00:00:43.040 --> 00:00:58.908
Step one, take total revenue. Step two, subtract total expenditure, and write the subtraction. For example, revenue fifty billion dollars, spending sixty billion. Fifty minus sixty is minus ten billion dollars.

00:00:59.480 --> 00:01:09.256
Step three, keep the sign. Here the spending bar is taller than the revenue bar, so the balance is negative. That is a deficit of ten billion dollars.

00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:18.093
Step four, copy the currency and units from the data, such as euros million, or rupees crore. Then say whether it is a deficit or a surplus.

00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:36.738
Here is a real question from June 2021. Figure 1 gives India's government revenue and expenditure for 2019 to 2020, in rupees crore. Calculate the fiscal surplus or deficit. Two marks.

00:01:37.320 --> 00:01:48.461
Revenue is two million, eighty thousand, two hundred and one crore. Expenditure is two million, seven hundred and eighty-four thousand, two hundred crore. Spending is bigger, so expect a deficit.

00:01:49.040 --> 00:02:02.333
Revenue minus expenditure gives minus seven hundred and three thousand, nine hundred and ninety-nine rupees crore. It is negative, so India had a fiscal deficit. This explanation is by olevels dot com, not an official mark scheme.

00:02:03.440 --> 00:02:12.748
A common mistake is subtracting the wrong way round. Spending minus revenue flips the sign, and turns a deficit into a surplus.

00:02:13.320 --> 00:02:20.481
Another is adding the two figures, or leaving out the currency and units. Subtract, then write the units.

00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:38.945
Now you try. In an example country, government revenue is eighty-four million euros, and government expenditure is seventy-one million euros. Calculate the fiscal balance and say whether it is a surplus or a deficit. Pause here and work it out.

00:02:40.080 --> 00:02:46.455
Revenue minus expenditure: eighty-four minus seventy-one is plus thirteen million euros.

00:02:47.040 --> 00:02:53.867
Revenue is the taller bar, so the balance is positive. This time it is a fiscal surplus of thirteen million euros.

00:02:54.440 --> 00:03:05.022
Remember: revenue minus expenditure, keep the sign, then add the units and name it. Negative is a deficit, positive is a surplus. Now try the practice questions.
